# Mutation cases for the Python half of a host-served Event I/O Processor.
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/host_processor_dispatch_python.cases`.
#
# Sibling of `host_processor_dispatch.cases` (Rust),
# `host_processor_dispatch_cpp.cases`, `host_processor_dispatch_c11.cases` and
# `host_processor_dispatch_go.cases`. The declaration half — the flag, the
# refusal, the manifest — is `host_processor_declaration.cases` and is not
# repeated here.
#
# ⚠ WHAT THIS FILE CANNOT REACH, said out loud rather than left to be
# discovered. The harness has two runners, `mutation_tests` (cargo) and
# `mutation_ctest`, and the Python suite is neither: `scripts/gates/w3c-python.sh`
# drives it with `pytest`. So the oracle below is the cargo-side channel test,
# and what it can judge is the EMITTED TEXT — that the dispatch is there, that
# the import it needs is there, that the author's `<param>` rides along, and
# that a machine declaring nothing carries neither.
#
# The runtime half — a registered handler being reached, an unregistered one
# raising `error.execution`, a reply becoming an event — is covered by
# `backends/python/tests/integration/host_processor/` and runs in the
# `w3c-python` gate, but no mutation round asks whether those tests could
# fail. A pytest runner for this harness is the repair, and it is the same
# one the Go casefile asks for; until it exists this file measures half the
# surface, and reading it as the whole one would be the same mistake the
# gates it mirrors exist to prevent.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --features cli --test host_processor_declaration
# `backends/python/runtime/` is deliberately NOT a target: a mutation there
# leaves every cargo test binary byte-identical, so the harness would report
# INCONCLUSIVE — a case that cannot run reads as coverage and measures
# nothing.
#
# Read at generation time rather than linked into the test binary, which is
# how the harness detects that a mutation arrived. `sce_codegen` is here
# because the CLI suite SPAWNS the binary, so mutating it changes
# `sce-codegen` and leaves every test binary identical.
mutation_runtime_targets tools/codegen/templates/python/actions/send.py.jinja2 \
    tools/codegen/templates/python/state_machine.py.jinja2 \
    tools/codegen/templates/python/invoke_methods.py.jinja2 \
    sce-build/src/generator.rs
mutation_oracles sce-build/tests/host_processor_declaration.rs

# ── The dispatch stops being emitted ────────────────────────────────
#
# The feature's off switch. Without the arm the send falls through to a
# target arm that knows nothing about the host, and the act reaches nobody.

mutation_case "the python template emits no host dispatch" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/python/actions/send.py.jinja2",
     "{% if action.send_type_host_served %}",
     "{% if false and action.send_type_host_served %}")
PY

# ── …or is emitted without what it needs to run ─────────────────────
#
# Python resolves names at call time, so a dispatch emitted without its
# import is a `NameError` at the first act — invisible to any build-time
# check and to every text assertion that only looks for the call.

mutation_case "the request type is not imported" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/python/state_machine.py.jinja2",
     "{% if model.host_processor_types %}\n# §scxml-6.2.5: imported only when this build was told the host serves a",
     "{% if false %}\n# §scxml-6.2.5: imported only when this build was told the host serves a")
PY

# ── …or is imported into every machine in the corpus ────────────────
#
# The other direction, and the cheapest way to make the case above pass.

mutation_case "the request type is imported unconditionally" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/python/state_machine.py.jinja2",
     "{% if model.host_processor_types %}\n# §scxml-6.2.5: imported only when this build was told the host serves a",
     "{% if true %}\n# §scxml-6.2.5: imported only when this build was told the host serves a")
PY

# ── …or arrives stripped ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A document that can name an act but not parameterise it has lost most of
# the reason to move the act into the document.

mutation_case "the params do not reach the request" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/python/actions/send.py.jinja2",
     """{{ indent }}_host_params = self._build_http_params(engine, [
{%- for param in action.params -%}
({{ param.name | py_string_literal }}, {{ param.expr | to_lua_expr | py_string_literal }}),
{%- endfor -%}
], {{ action.namelist | py_string_literal }})""",
     "{{ indent }}_host_params = {}")
PY

# ── The declared `<invoke>` stops being started ─────────────────────
#
# ⚠ THIS SECTION USED TO MUTATE A REFUSAL — `python accepts an invoker
# declaration it cannot service`, which deleted Python's
# `reject_host_invokers_in_unsupported_lang` call. Python grew an invoker
# registry, the whole fail-fast was retired, and the case had nothing left to
# delete. Not re-aimed: the site was removed on purpose and the claim it
# guarded ("Python cannot service this") is now false, so the case could only
# ever pass. What replaces it is the claim the retirement makes instead —
# Python DOES start what the host declared — in the same two directions the
# send half above is measured in.

mutation_case "the python template emits no host invoke start" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/python/invoke_methods.py.jinja2",
     "{% for inv in state.invokes | host_invoker %}",
     "{% for inv in [] %}")
PY

mutation_case "a declared invoke also takes the refusal arm" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/python/invoke_methods.py.jinja2",
     "{% for inv in state.invokes | unsupported_refused %}",
     "{% for inv in (state.invokes | unsupported_refused | list) + (state.invokes | host_invoker | list) %}")
PY
